    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, thank you. To follow up on the pH aspect, I'm looking at how this applies to different cooking mediums. When you're searing a steak, for example, the surface moisture and the local pH at the interface can be quite variable. Do you think the presence of specific organic acids—like lactic acid in meat—serves as a significant buffer that suppresses the Maillard reaction during the early stages of heat application? And how would you describe the transition from that initial glycosylamine formation to the more complex stage where those melanoidins actually start to develop their characteristic aroma and color? I want to make sure I'm characterizing the \"jump\" in complexity correctly in my paper.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
